README.md:131:### Offline mode (Gemma 3 1B on device)
README.md:133:The cloud path stays the default. When the browser is offline, or a POST to `/api/turn` or `/api/correct` fails with a network error (fetch rejects, not a 4xx/5xx), the client answers with Gemma 3 1B instruct running in the browser through the MediaPipe LLM Inference API (`@mediapipe/tasks-genai`). Replies from the device carry `source: "local"` and Roleplay shows "offline, noé runs on this device".
README.md:135:What works offline:
README.md:145:1. On Hugging Face, open `litert-community/Gemma3-1B-IT`, accept the Gemma licence (the repo is gated) and download `gemma3-1b-it-int4-web.task` (about 700 MB).
README.md:146:2. In alltag, open Settings, section "offline tutor", and choose that file. It is copied into the Origin Private File System and loaded from there; the app never downloads it on its own.
README.md:147:3. Optional: set `VITE_GEMMA_MODEL_URL` to a URL you host the file at; Settings then shows a "download" button instead of the file picker.
README.md:153:The native app ships the model in its bundle, so a TestFlight or App Store install has the offline tutor ready with no file picking.
README.md:160:- `public/models/gemma3-1b-it-int4-web.task` lands in `dist/` and is served at `https://localhost/models/...` inside the app. On start `src/offline/gemma.ts` sends a HEAD there; a 200 loads the model straight from that URL and Settings shows "ready, built into the app".
README.md:161:- The file ships inside the IPA (about 700 MB), so the App Store marks the app as a large download.
README.md:163:- Licence: Gemma is distributed under the [Gemma Terms of Use](https://ai.google.dev/gemma/terms). The app must show that notice and link the [prohibited use policy](https://ai.google.dev/gemma/prohibited_use_policy); Settings shows "on-device model: Gemma 3 1B, Gemma Terms of Use" whenever a model is bundled or installed.
README.md:168:The app ships a service worker, so once installed it opens with no network and the offline tutor is reachable.
README.md:192:- Limits: WebGPU inside WKWebView on iOS 26 is untested, so the on-device Gemma fallback may not work in the native shell. The PWA on Android Chrome is the tested offline path.
README.md:196:Types are in `src/lib/types.ts` (`CheckRequest`/`CheckResponse` in `src/lib/api.ts`). Full examples and error codes in [docs/API.md](docs/API.md).
docs/ARCHITECTURE.md:46:`vite-plugin-pwa` (workbox `generateSW`) builds `dist/sw.js` with `registerType: "autoUpdate"` (the worker skips waiting and claims clients); `src/main.tsx` registers `/sw.js` itself in production builds, since the plugin's `virtual:pwa-register` would pull in `workbox-window`. It precaches the app shell (`**/*.{js,css,html,svg,woff2}`, files up to 5 MB) and answers navigations with `/index.html`, so the installed app loads with no network. `/api/*` is excluded from the navigate fallback and routed `NetworkOnly`: API responses are never cached, and offline the client falls back to the on-device model instead. The manifest stays `public/manifest.webmanifest` (`manifest: false` in the plugin).
docs/ARCHITECTURE.md:48:## Offline model sources
docs/ARCHITECTURE.md:50:`src/offline/gemma.ts` reports where the Gemma model came from in `status().source`. `bundled`: the native app serves `public/models/gemma3-1b-it-int4-web.task` from its own origin; one HEAD to `/models/...` per session decides it, and MediaPipe loads that URL directly (no OPFS). `opfs`: the human picked the file in Settings and it was copied into the Origin Private File System. `url`: same OPFS copy, downloaded from `VITE_GEMMA_MODEL_URL`. A 404, a network error or a non-http origin skips the bundled source, so the web deployment (which never gets the file) behaves as before. The precache ignores `*.task` and `*.litertlm`.

